  "tensions": [
    "If VALIS is physical and must arrange bits to think, how can it be mind showing through reality? (Item 3 perplexes PKD despite 'Matter is plastic in the face of mind')",
    "How can there be plural sacred histories underlying one world unless VALIS runs simultaneous programs? (Logical puzzle driving PKD back to square one)",
    "Is Thomas tracking an alternate world-program or the sacred narrative ground of PKD's present world? PKD resolves toward phylogenic memory but remains uncertain.",
    "Could PKD have unconsciously known Acts, so the novel's Acts material is coincidence rather than proof of alternate narrative? Yet John Taverner (d. 1530) is unknown consciously, making dual coincidence near-impossible.",
    "Is the ghostly TEARS-outline the remote past or an alternate coaxial world? PKD shifts position mid-text, revising earlier certainty.",
    "If there are multiple sacred narratives with no single noumenal ground, what unifies these realities? PKD notes they are 'somehow superimposed, as if on one axis' but lacks a unifying principle."
  ],
  "evidence_quotes": [
    "VALIS is running human history programs, several of them (my tracks A, B and C); these are parallel Earths and differ from each other, and when in 2-3-74 I saw the world of TEARS I did see an alternate world.",
    "Christianity is not the truth but only one 'sacred narrative,' which is to say one sacred history out of a plural number.",
    "The mythic ritual is an entry-key into the sacred narrative. It functions the way an entry-key of a computer functions vis-à-vis a given program.",
    "It might be a coincidence that either (2) or (3) was there by coincidence but not both. Clearly, there is an Urstory, which I call a 'sacred narrative,' the informational basis of reality; or: it is an alternate reality seeping through.",
    "I punched into Christianity because of the particular mythic rite I reenacted; had I reenacted another mythic rite I would have punched into another 'sacred narrative,' which is to say program."
  ],
